1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,910
A hundred waits for a bus: mostly short, with a long tail.

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:07,920
Textbooks say the average of thirty is safe to treat as a bell.

3
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:12,150
For these waits, does it take thirty, three hundred, or over a thousand?

4
00:00:13,766 --> 00:00:20,356
A-B tests lean on that bell's tails, and on revenue data, thirty is off by a factor of thousands.

5
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,530
Maybe thirty just means collecting enough waits.

6
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:27,160
Collect a thousand, ten thousand, a hundred thousand.

7
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:30,200
The bars get smoother, but the shape doesn't budge.

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:38,010
Measure the lean with skewness: the average cubed gap from the mean, left gaps counting negative, over the spread cubed.

9
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:39,900
A perfect bell scores zero.

10
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:43,920
These waits for a bus score about two at every sample size.

11
00:00:44,733 --> 00:00:46,133
So average instead.

12
00:00:46,633 --> 00:00:51,143
Four bus waits: the one long wait gets diluted by three short ones.

13
00:00:51,943 --> 00:00:53,613
Drop that one number here.

14
00:00:53,763 --> 00:00:55,613
Do that ten thousand times.

15
00:00:55,766 --> 00:00:58,446
These averages lean less: skewness one.

16
00:00:58,596 --> 00:01:00,856
Sixteen per average: point five.

17
00:01:01,006 --> 00:01:05,126
Sixty-four: point two five, nearly this perfect bell in green.

18
00:01:05,333 --> 00:01:12,653
Each time the group grows four times, the skewness halves: two over root n, and two is the skewness of one wait.

19
00:01:13,466 --> 00:01:15,106
The root comes from the cubes.

20
00:01:15,256 --> 00:01:19,616
Add four bus waits: their cubed gaps add up to four times one wait's.

21
00:01:19,766 --> 00:01:23,256
But the spread only doubles, so its cube grows eight times.

22
00:01:23,406 --> 00:01:25,326
Four over eight: half the lean.

23
00:01:25,476 --> 00:01:28,286
In general, n over n to the one and a half.

24
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,630
Kurtosis, the extra tail weight, fades faster: it quarters each time.

25
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:37,300
Every finer difference fades faster still.

26
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:38,920
Only the bell survives.

27
00:01:39,070 --> 00:01:40,940
That's the central limit theorem.

28
00:01:41,090 --> 00:01:44,500
It needs independent pieces, and one more condition.

29
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,000
Back to the textbook's thirty.

30
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,190
Two over root thirty: point three seven.

31
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:51,820
Close to a bell, by eye.

32
00:01:52,033 --> 00:01:58,783
But tests live in the tails: a ninety-five percent interval should miss two and a half percent on each side.

33
00:01:58,933 --> 00:02:01,503
Tests estimate the spread from the same data.

34
00:02:01,653 --> 00:02:07,653
With lopsided waits, a low average comes with a small spread, so its interval stops short.

35
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:12,103
With thirty waits: seven percent low, one percent high.

36
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:16,740
Ron Kohavi and colleagues want each side between two and three percent.

37
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:21,230
Their rule: n at least three hundred fifty-five times the skewness squared.

38
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:23,480
Divide by n and take the root:

39
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:28,720
it's our formula: the average's skewness at most point oh five three.

40
00:02:28,870 --> 00:02:32,450
At three hundred, the lean is point one two: still too much.

41
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:38,460
For these waits: fourteen twenty, lean point oh five three, and both misses reach the band.

42
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:45,930
Bing's revenue per user had a skewness near eighteen: a hundred and fourteen thousand users.

43
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:50,080
Capping each user at ten dollars a week: under ten thousand.

44
00:02:50,366 --> 00:02:53,736
And for some data, no number on this axis is enough.

45
00:02:54,833 --> 00:03:01,963
A lighthouse one mile off a straight shore flashes at random angles, and each flash lands somewhere on the shore.

46
00:03:02,166 --> 00:03:04,046
Track the average landing spot.

47
00:03:04,196 --> 00:03:10,496
It never settles: a flash aimed almost along the shore lands absurdly far away, and yanks it.

48
00:03:10,966 --> 00:03:16,306
Among a thousand flashes, the farthest typically lands about nine hundred miles off.

49
00:03:16,456 --> 00:03:20,656
Divided by a thousand, it still drags the average about a mile.

50
00:03:20,806 --> 00:03:24,336
Half of single flashes land within a mile of the middle.

51
00:03:24,486 --> 00:03:29,196
Repeat the thousand-flash average two hundred times, and still only half do.

52
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,780
The theorem needs finite variance: no single piece can outweigh the rest.

53
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:36,780
Here the variance is infinite.

54
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:40,800
The median ignores how far the extremes fly, so it homes in.

55
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:44,943
So the answer is over a thousand, not thirty.

56
00:03:45,093 --> 00:03:47,353
Collect forever: still lopsided.

57
00:03:47,503 --> 00:03:49,323
The bell was never in the data.

58
00:03:49,473 --> 00:03:50,813
It's in the average.

